  "_committedFixtureNotes": "This is the COMMITTED, public-repo version of scratchpad/chiefBenchmark.json (anonymized chief hand-built schedule, block 2026-07-27..2026-08-23). One deliberate adjustment from the scratchpad source, documented per src/lib/chiefBenchmark.test.js: the 7 SAMMC-mapped offServiceResidents entries (category EM_BAMC, pgy 2 or 3 in the ids bench_offsvc_sammc_pgy2_a..d/bench_offsvc_sammc_pgy3_a..c) have their pgy field set to 1 here. BASE_ELIGIBILITY only defines an EM_BAMC_1 key (no EM_BAMC_2/EM_BAMC_3) -- leaving these residents at pgy 2/3 would give them an EMPTY eligibility list under the current app rules and make every one of their historical shift assignments a spurious \"not eligible\" validateAll error, drowning out the real signal this fixture exists to measure. IDs keep their original pgy2/pgy3 label for traceability back to the source data (see notes.md's \"Off-service category mapping\" section); only the pgy FIELD used by the scheduling engine changed. No other data was altered. IMPORTANT, added after a live chief-verification session: this fixture's `block.schedule` contains WORKED CALL-IN shifts that are indistinguishable from ordinary scheduled ones -- only the roster's own `Res_Call PGY*` rows were separated out into `jeopardyDates`. The chief's own words: any shift outside a resident's normal window/rotation, or in a non-peds area for an EM/EMS or EM/TOX PGY-2 (whose real scheduled work is exactly their 8 window days, ~100% PEDS-area), is generally a call-in they were pulled in for, not a planned assignment -- and a resident with fewer shifts than expected is generally paying back a shift for someone else's call-in. Per-resident shift COUNTS read off this fixture's `block.schedule` must therefore never be used to derive or validate shift targets, and small over/under-target deviations measured against it are expected noise, not a bug in either the fixture or the app's rules."
}
